}

\p{\strong{The 15 exports that \code{\verb~|skipargs~} / \code{\verb~|congruence~} accept and the official checker rejects} (the 13 rows of S-F1 / S-F2, \code{\verb~|open-flag-closed-bad~}, \code{\verb~|acc-rec-intro-vs-axiom~}) are REJECTED under every switch set with \code{\verb~|m4b~} that they were run under (\code{\verb~|a4~}, \code{\verb~|f4~}, \code{\verb~|rich4~}, \code{\verb~|all4~}, \code{\verb~|dflt4~}, \code{\verb~|dflt4j4~}). The control: on the same binary \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~} accepts two of my 41 exports that the official checker rejects (\code{\verb~|fuse-abbrev-samehead-proofarg-bad~}, \code{\verb~|fuse-opaque-samehead-proofarg-bad~}): the suite does see the class. Raw rows: \code{\verb~|work/c1/out/judge-*.tsv~}.}

\p{Fast test group of the module with the patch applied (the clean tree, \code{\verb~|mvn test -Dgroups=fast~}): exit 0.}

\p{The 41 new exports (\code{\verb~|work/c1/hostile/~}, generator \code{\verb~|work/c1/gen/hostile.py~}, manifest with the official verdicts \code{\verb~|work/c1/hostile/manifest.tsv~}; my prediction of the official verdict was right for all 41):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Group}\th{Exports}\th{What must hold}}
\tr{\td{same REGULAR definition, arguments equal only by proof irrelevance (\code{\verb~|G I = G h~}, both orders)}\td{2 ok}\td{accepted: the arguments are compared first; a fused both-unfold would reject}}
\tr{\td{same ABBREV / OPAQUE definition, the same arguments}\td{2 bad}\td{rejected: both unfold, \code{\verb~|And.rec f I~} is 5, \code{\verb~|And.rec f h~} is stuck (accepted by \code{\verb~|congruence~})}}
\tr{\td{unequal hints (abbrev over regular, regular over abbrev, height 2 over height 1)}\td{3 ok}\td{accepted: one side unfolds first and meets the other; unfolding both would reject}}
\tr{\td{two regular definitions of one height}\td{1 ok, 1 bad}\td{both unfold without an argument comparison}}
\tr{\td{projection functions with hint abbrev / opaque / regular over two structure definitions; two projection functions; constructor struct; struct equal by structure eta on one side; two axioms; arguments by unfolding}\td{9 ok, 7 bad}\td{the chain unfold, stuck projection, second \code{\verb~|whnf_core~}, restart}}
\tr{\td{a projection function of a PROOF field applied to an argument}\td{1 ok, 1 bad twin}\td{the application is a proof: by its type, never unfolded}}
\tr{\td{a parameter that differs as a term and is dropped by the unfolding}\td{1 ok}\td{identical after the step: the quick test}}
\tr{\td{the shape of \code{\verb~|HMul.hMul~} over \code{\verb~|instHMul~} over \code{\verb~|Mul.mul~}, also nested}\td{2 ok, 2 bad}\td{two fused levels, the CORE memo on the second occurrence}}
\tr{\td{Nat functions, closed}\td{1 ok, 1 bad}\td{folded, not unfolded (the shape excludes them)}}
\tr{\td{wrappers that unfold to recursor applications / to constructor applications (also differing in a proof field)}\td{3 ok, 1 bad}\td{a result that the next state can still change goes to the complete judgment}}
\tr{\td{a projection chain at a type of sort \code{\verb~|Sort (imax 1 0)~} whose unfolding has a type of declared sort \code{\verb~|Prop~}, once / twice in two declarations / twice in one}\td{3 ok}\td{proof irrelevance before and after the unfolding (see 8)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|a = b~} for two axioms of \code{\verb~|Q : Sort (imax 1 0)~} and of \code{\verb~|Q.{1} : Sort (imax u 0)~}}\td{2 ok}\td{probes of the base: the official \code{\verb~|is_prop~} normalises the level (accept), and so do both engines}}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A mechanised argument for the CORE memo's answer to a cheap query (3.1).} It lets the lazy loop continue where the official algorithm restarts \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_core~}, so the proof-irrelevance test and the \code{\verb~|Bool.true~} test of the restarted pair are not made on a repeated chain. That is exact if "is a proof" cannot change along an unfolding. In the pinned kernel it cannot, by the standard meta-theory: \code{\verb~|is_prop~} is \code{\verb~|normalizes_to_zero~} of the sort of the type (\code{\verb~|type_checker.cpp~} 379-385, not a syntactic comparison with \code{\verb~|Prop~}), the types of a term and of its reduct are convertible (subject reduction), hence their sorts carry equivalent levels. I tried to separate the two paths with a type of sort \code{\verb~|Sort (imax 1 0)~} behind a definition of declared type \code{\verb~|Prop~} (three \code{\verb~|restart-proof-*~} exports, two \code{\verb~|isprop-imax-*~} probes): the official checker treats both as propositions and every configuration agrees with it. No counter-example, no mechanised proof. This concerns the published machine with every switch set, not \code{\verb~|m4b~} (which follows the complete judgment); owners: M3 (\code{\verb~|Reduce.core~}), M4, package S.}
\li{\strong{A proof of the meta-theorem of 4.3.} I could not prove it and found no export that refutes its restricted form (identical arguments, data only); so "a lockstep walk that is exact for propositions without a proof-inspecting reduction" is neither established nor refuted. What I can say is that it would not apply to the hot comparisons (their leaves are equal only up to proof irrelevance).}
\li{\strong{The size of 3.4} is an estimate from counters; nothing of it was built.}
\li{\strong{A CPU effect of \code{\verb~|m4b~} below 1 \%}: the two windows resolve about 1 \% of the checking time; the step shows none.}
\li{\strong{All of Mathlib}: counters and CPU time are from two slices (\code{\verb~|Scheme.cone~}, \code{\verb~|Galois.Basic.cone~}) and the Cedar slice; all of Mathlib was run once, for the wall clock with eight workers (6.2), not for counters or per-unit CPU time. The shares by class of unit are those of \code{\verb~|Scheme.cone~}.}
\li{\strong{Parallel workers}: the counters and the CPU table are one worker; four workers were run on the hostile exports (\code{\verb~|dflt4j4~}) and in the pipeline part of the gates, eight in 6.2. No export targets a race in the step (it has no state of its own: nothing to race on).}
\li{The official kernel's own cost on these units was not measured (P8: 30 times gruszka's instructions on Mathlib).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Deliverables and what each owner would do with them}

\table{
\tr{\th{What}\th{Where}\th{Owner}}
\tr{\td{patch: switch \code{\verb~|m4b~}, the fused two-sided lazy step (\code{\verb~|Conv.java~} only: \code{\verb~|convFuse~}, \code{\verb~|fuseShape~}, \code{\verb~|fuseQuick~}, frames \code{\verb~|CONV2[9..12]~}; applies to the main tree of 21:48Z, whose \code{\verb~|Conv.java~} is that of the copy)}\td{\code{\verb~|work/c1/patches/0001-fused-two-sided-lazy-step.M4.patch~}}\td{M4}}
\tr{\td{41 hostile exports with official verdicts (leanchecker 4.36.0, arena container) and their generator}\td{\code{\verb~|work/c1/hostile/~}, \code{\verb~|manifest.tsv~}; \code{\verb~|work/c1/gen/hostile.py~}}\td{candidates for package S}}
\tr{\td{gate reports of the clean private tree}\td{\code{\verb~|work/c1/clean/work/status/machine-gate-c1-*.md~}, \code{\verb~|machine-conv-c1b*.txt~}}\td{M8 (finding C1-F2)}}
\tr{\td{tools: per-unit tap and sequential trace (private hooks), counters, judge, timing}\td{\code{\verb~|work/c1/run.sh~}, \code{\verb~|spans.py~}, \code{\verb~|view.py~}, \code{\verb~|sum.py~}, \code{\verb~|judge.py~}, \code{\verb~|quiet-cpu.sh~}, \code{\verb~|quiet-wall.sh~}, \code{\verb~|qtable.py~}}\td{-}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{M4}: the step is the two-sided case of \code{\verb~|lstepBoth~} inlined; with the switch off \code{\verb~|convLazy~} tests one bit. It counts a fused iteration as a probe event B1 / T: it wants a counter of its own. Its value is small (section 6): take it with the next gate of the default set, not for its own sake.}
\li{\strong{M6}: \code{\verb~|m4b~} is M4's free experiment bit (the earlier \code{\verb~|m4b~} experiment was removed, NOTES-M4 W5); no change to \code{\verb~|Op~}, \code{\verb~|Debug~} or \code{\verb~|Machine~} is needed. A name is yours to give.}
\li{\strong{M3 / M4 / package S}: the argument of 3.1 / section 8 for the CORE memo was not written down anywhere; five exports now sit next to it.}
\li{\strong{M8 / M7 / the integrator}: finding C1-F2 (7.1).}
\li{\strong{The owner}: the comparison of proofs the official way is a cost of the official algorithm, not of this implementation. The decision of P8's section 0, point 5, stands as it was: official verdicts at this price, or a named profile with the two theory-sound options. This report removes the third possibility.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. Reproduction}

\pre{\verb~|
C=work/c1
bench/slot.sh -- $C/build.sh                        # compile the private tree (with the trace hooks) and the tap
C1_ONLY=AlgebraicGeometry.Spec.sheafedSpaceMap_hom_c_app bench/slot.sh -- $C/run.sh base scheme warm,gated,expect
awk '/^--- first/{f=1;next} f' $C/data/trace-base-scheme.txt > $C/data/seq-base.txt
python3 -I $C/spans.py $C/data/seq-base.txt 9 1500 210      # nested spans with their ticks
python3 -I $C/view.py $C/data/seq-base.txt 3421 3640        # one proof's type comparison, event by event
bench/slot.sh -- $C/batch2.sh; python3 -I $C/sum.py scheme a a4 af af4 richx rich4 nx nx4 nxr   # the counter table
bench/slot.sh -- $C/mkclean.sh                      # the tree without the hooks, the tap, the patch per owner
python3 -I $C/gen/hostile.py; $C/official.sh        # the new hostile exports and their official verdicts (Docker)
bench/slot.sh -- $C/hostile-all.sh                  # S, S2 and C1 exports under the m4b switch sets
$C/gates.sh                                         # M8's gate on the clean tree (reports: $C/clean/work/status/)
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- $C/nbuild.sh            # native -O3 of the clean tree: $C/dist-n
bench/quiet.py -- $C/quiet-cpu.sh scheme 2          # thread CPU of the checks, 1 worker
bench/quiet.py -- $C/quiet-wall.sh scheme 4         # wall clock of every pass, 8 workers, pipelined
~}
}
